<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Little St Bernard Pass</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Little St Bernard Pass is an Alpine pass at about 2,188 m between the Tarentaise valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savoie">Savoie</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aosta-valley">Aosta Valley</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Used since prehistory and controlled by the Ceutrones and Salassi, it carried a Roman road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aosta">Augusta Praetoria</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, and some historians argue that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> by it in 218 BCE. A hospice for travellers was founded there in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/little-st-bernard-pass.jpg" alt="Little St Bernard Pass" width="320" height="167" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ²°¹°° at French Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Col_du_Petit-Saint-Bernard_-_cromlech.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
